# Trivy misconfiguration suppressions. Each entry carries its reason: a
# suppression without one is indistinguishable from a gate someone silenced
# because it was inconvenient.
#
# SCOPE, read this before adding a Dockerfile. `.trivyignore` has no path
# syntax: an ID listed here is suppressed REPO-WIDE, for every file Trivy
# scans, not only the one its reason talks about. That is acceptable today for
# exactly one reason — this repo contains exactly ONE Dockerfile
# (.github/senzing-eval/Dockerfile, the ephemeral eval fixture), so repo-wide
# and file-specific are currently the same set. Verify with:
#     find . -name 'Dockerfile*' -not -path './node_modules/*'
# The moment a SECOND Dockerfile lands — and especially a shipped or
# network-facing one — these two entries stop being scoped to a test fixture
# and start hiding real findings. Do not leave them as they are: delete them
# and re-run Trivy, or move the suppressions into per-file `#trivy:ignore:<ID>`
# comments inside the fixture Dockerfile itself, which is the only mechanism
# that actually scopes by path.

# .github/senzing-eval/Dockerfile — ephemeral CI test fixture, never deployed,
# never network-facing, run --privileged by necessity (the sandbox that
# `claude plugin eval` needs for its Bash grant requires an unprivileged user
# namespace). Switching to a non-root USER is a genuine improvement and is
# tracked as a follow-up, but it moves /work ownership and the sandbox HOME and
# there is no Docker daemon available to verify it. Shipping an untested
# container change to satisfy a scanner trades a documented risk for an
# undocumented one.
AVD-DS-0002

# A HEALTHCHECK is meaningless for a batch container that runs one command and
# exits — nothing orchestrates it and nothing consumes the status. Adding a
# no-op probe to turn a scanner green is exactly the gate-that-cannot-fail
# pattern this PR exists to remove.
AVD-DS-0026
